CloudBurrow patch 59 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1508).

The bug: COLLATE in DDL (`s STRING COLLATE 'und:ci'`, a table's DEFAULT
COLLATE) and the COLLATE function failed 400 "COLLATE is not supported",
and a STRING column with the collation 'und:ci' compared by code point:
`WHERE s = 'aBC'` did not match 'Abc', and GROUP BY, DISTINCT, ORDER BY,
joins, MIN/MAX and COUNT(DISTINCT) ignored the collation.

The fix, as BigQuery's collation reference documents
(https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/collation-concepts):
- the analyzer and the catalog's builtins have the annotation framework
  and collation support on, so COLLATE analyzes, the analyzer's own
  COLLATE function replaces googlesqlite's stand-in (which gave its
  result no collation), and the analyzer propagates collation and
  refuses what the reference refuses (two different collations
  compared);
- a CREATE TABLE records each STRING column's collation (its COLLATE, or
  the table's DEFAULT COLLATE) in ColumnSpec.Collation, and the catalog
  gives the column that collation as an annotation (needs go-googlesql
  patch 0003);
- the formatter compares und:ci values case-folded: a comparison's
  STRING arguments (=, !=, <, BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, STARTS_WITH, ...) through
  LOWER, GROUP BY (and so DISTINCT) on the folded key, ORDER BY by a new
  SQLite collation, googlesqlite_collate_ci, MIN and MAX as MIN_BY and
  MAX_BY of the folded value (so the value itself is returned), and
  COUNT(DISTINCT) of the folded value.
Its test collation_test.go fails without it.

